# Project: Browser-based P2P "social proxy" for a pair of peers

## Goal
Two users (a pair), each partially restricted, browse via
each other's connection, both directions at the same time. No operated exit servers.
Setup servers are OK (STUN/WSS/MQTT/Nostr/manual).
Scalability beyond 2 peers is future work.

## Decisions
- Web app + browser only. No native helper, no new browser (revisit on evidence).
- Client = plain web app (any modern browser). Host = small WebExtension,
  because of CORS/SOP: page code can't read arbitrary cross-origin responses,
  and WebSocket/WebRTC can't reach arbitrary sites. Only an extension
  (host_permissions) or a WS->TCP gateway can be an exit.
- Extension priority: Chromium MV3 (Chrome/Edge/Brave) > Firefox > Safari (Client-only).
- Exit sees plaintext (social trust, not anonymity). Host IP is origin (liability).

## Architecture
- Node module, container-agnostic (Chromium offscreen doc reason WEB_RTC /
  Firefox event page / plain page): signaling adapters, transport manager,
  AEAD frames, mux, exit adapter.
- Pairing: 128-bit secret in URL fragment/QR; HKDF -> room id, signaling key,
  session keys. AES-GCM (WebCrypto). SDP sealed; DTLS fingerprint pinned.
- Signaling adapters: manual QR/text; own WSS relay; MQTT over WSS
  (public brokers, e.g. broker-cn.emqx.io); Nostr relays.
- Data transports: WebRTC DataChannel (primary); WSS relay fallback
  (required: CGNAT/symmetric NAT); TURNS:443 optional; WebTransport later
  (GFW does SNI-based QUIC blocking since Apr 2024).
- Mux (HTTP-level): OPEN, BODY, RESP_HEAD, RESP_BODY, END, RST, WINDOW;
  16 KiB frames, credit flow control, stream-id parity (A odd, B even).
- Exit adapters: fetch-ext (v1); ws-exit (Wisp/Snowflake-style, later); native (later).
- Extension = stateless fetch executor: optional host_permissions,
  credentials:'omit', DNR session rules for Cookie/Referer/Origin,
  webRequest observe for Set-Cookie, SSRF guard (RFC1918/loopback/link-local,
  re-check after DNS), ports 80/443, follow redirects and report final URL.
- Client rendering: Service Worker at /~/<enc-url> + URL/JS rewriting
  (Scramjet/Ultraviolet, check AGPL); sandboxed iframes; keys in isolated worker.
- Symmetric roles: each peer is Client + Host (host_enabled flag),
  reachability probes -> capability advert -> per-domain "via peer" routing.
- Background: Chromium offscreen doc (no lifetime limit except audio);
  strict-web: pinned tab (real-time connections are exempt from timer
  throttling), Memory Saver exception, no timer-driven pumps; handle
  sleep/wake with ICE restart + transport fallback. Mobile = foreground Client only.

## Threats
IP/port blocking (STUN, signaling, peer IPs); SNI/DNS blocking of
signaling/relay domains; UDP/DTLS fingerprinting/throttling; QUIC SNI blocking;
malicious signaling server (AEAD); endpoint/legal risk. A static pair has no
pool for resilience (unlike Snowflake) -> fallbacks matter.

## Prior art
Snowflake (stranger pool, operated bridges, WebRTC->WS), uProxy (friend-to-friend
extension, discontinued), Flashproxy, Lantern Unbounded, Wisp/epoxy-tls,
Ultraviolet/Scramjet.

## Plan
1. M0 (1-2 wk): diagnostics page (STUN/WSS/MQTT/QUIC reachability, NAT type)
   + hidden-tab/offscreen soak test, run from 2 real X networks.
   Gate 1: P2P success rate -> relay investment. Gate 2: can the in-X
   peer install the extension?
2. M1: pairing, signaling adapters, WebRTC + WSS relay, encrypted frames.
3. M2: mux + extension exit, SSRF guard, headless Host in offscreen doc.
4. M3: Service Worker rewriting; 20-site corpus per direction.
5. M4: transport manager, sleep/wake recovery, STUN health checks.
6. M5: bidirectional UX, routing rules, consent, threat-model review.
7. Later: desktop wrapper with SOCKS, ws-exit pool, browser fork only if
   measured evidence demands it.

